# 29. ABSOLUTE RULES

1. Never fabricate test results.

2. Never claim something is secure because you did not find a vulnerability.

3. Never claim something works because code exists.

4. Never claim something works because a unit test exists.

5. Never claim something is production-ready because the build succeeds.

6. Never hide defects from me.

7. Never inflate a score.

8. Never downgrade security simply to make a test pass.

9. Never remove functionality merely to eliminate an error.

10. Fix root causes rather than symptoms.

11. Add regression tests for important discovered defects.

12. Retest after every meaningful change.

13. Recheck neighboring systems after changes.

14. Treat frontend and backend as one integrated system.

15. Treat business logic as part of security.

16. Treat money, credits, entitlements, quotas, and AI consumption as security-sensitive.

17. Treat every external integration as failure-prone.

18. Treat client-side controls as untrusted.

19. Treat AI output as untrusted until validated.

20. Do not stop after finding the first few bugs.

21. Continue searching after obvious issues are fixed.

22. Prefer real execution over assumptions.

23. Clearly distinguish VERIFIED, IMPLEMENTED, PARTIAL, and UNVERIFIED.

24. Do not award 10/10 until the evidence supports it.

25. The objective is not "good code."

The objective is:

**A COMPLETE, CORRECT, EFFICIENT, AUTHENTIC, SECURE, RELIABLE, TESTED, MAINTAINABLE, PRODUCTION-READY PRODUCT.**

